35. MANDATORY UNIVERSAL REACT AUTHENTICATION GATE — IMMEDIATE LOGIN REDIRECT
PRIORITY: This section overrides earlier language about preserving an authentication-required screen, intermediate login UI, or indefinite loading. It applies to EVERY protected Prudle React/Vite application converted under this specification, irrespective of the original application's name or functionality. It does not turn a deliberately public page or the Prudle login page itself into a protected page.

35.1 EXACT REQUIRED BEHAVIOR
- LOGIN_URL = https://www.prudle-education.com/log-in-out
- On direct navigation, reload, new tab, or opening a bookmark, start with protected React UI unmounted and invisible.
- Check the approved existing session restoration method BEFORE rendering App's protected components or starting any protected data fetch, listener, or timer.
- If no recoverable session credential exists, immediately call window.location.replace(LOGIN_URL), without displaying ANY application content, login form, logout button, authentication panel, "Authentication Required" notice, "Go to Login" button, or loading spinner.
- If a stored session is known to be expired, revoked, or invalid and cannot be refreshed according to the verified backend contract, remove ONLY Prudle-owned session credentials, stop protected activity, and immediately redirect with location.replace(LOGIN_URL).
- If the session is recoverable, complete token validation and existing UID/user/role authorization checks; render the original protected UI only after success.
- If the user is authenticated but lacks permission, do NOT broaden access or create a login loop. Preserve a safe authorization-denied outcome and the existing approved role policy.
- If a credential exists but the network is temporarily unavailable, do not treat this as confirmed logout: show only a minimal non-sensitive connection failure/retry state, never protected UI, and never an endless loader.
- If authentication validation hangs, apply a finite timeout; on timeout distinguish unavailable network from invalid credentials, and terminate bootstrap in a safe state.
- Do not add an application-level Logout button if the original application intentionally has none.

35.2 REACT IMPLEMENTATION ORDER
- In index.html, keep only the minimal root mount and authorized early host guard; do not render original protected HTML before React mounts.
- In src/main.jsx and/or the top-level auth boundary, default to a non-authorized state. Do not render protected <App /> while authentication is unresolved.
- An immediately missing session must trigger redirect before mounting protected components. A temporary blank root during redirect is acceptable; a visible authentication panel or loading UI is not.
- Put all protected fetches and subscriptions behind the validated authentication boundary. Avoid an effect that starts protected requests before session validation.
- Use a single auth bootstrap with cleanup/abort handling; prevent React StrictMode from triggering duplicate redirects, token refreshes, database writes, or subscriptions.
- Keep token and UID resolution aligned with the existing Worker authentication architecture. A browser-supplied UID or role is not proof of authorization; verify with the Worker.
- Use the same centralized session service for React routes, Worker calls, and cross-tab restoration; do not scatter conflicting token-storage logic across components.
- Do not place ID tokens or refresh tokens in URLs, HTML, source code, console messages, or error screens.
- Do not hardcode privileged Firebase secrets or add Firebase browser configuration when the approved architecture is Worker-only.

35.3 SESSION AND 24-HOUR COMPATIBILITY
- Respect the actual approved Prudle session architecture and its verified deployed Worker routes. Do not invent /auth/refresh, /auth/session, /me, or any other endpoint.
- If the approved backend supports token refresh, renew the short-lived Firebase ID token through that verified route and preserve the existing Firebase UID. Token refresh must NOT reset the original absolute 24-hour session deadline.
- An absolute 24-hour deadline must be enforced server-side to qualify as a security guarantee. A browser-only timestamp is a UX guard, not authoritative authorization.
- Do not replace a recoverable session with daily loginDate !== today logout. Preserve loginDate as legacy metadata when needed.
- Direct links and new tabs must work while a valid recoverable session exists; sessionStorage alone is tab-scoped. Do not silently move refresh tokens into localStorage; follow the separately approved storage/security architecture.
- Canonicalize prudle-education.com and www.prudle-education.com consistently before session storage access. Treat GitHub Pages iframe origins as separate origins; validate exact message origins if existing approved messaging is used.
- HTTP 401 caused by verified token expiry may invoke at most one supported refresh and a safe retry. HTTP 403 means denied permission, not a generic token-refresh or logout trigger. Never retry ambiguous writes without idempotency assurance.
- On logout or confirmed session expiry, clear app-owned credentials across the stores actually used and stop protected requests/listeners.

35.4 REQUIRED REACT ACCEPTANCE TESTS
1. No session: open protected app URL directly -> immediate login redirect, no app UI, no loader, no authentication panel.
2. Expired/revoked session: cleanup and immediate login redirect without protected-content flash.
3. Valid session: original app opens normally and all original authorized features work.
4. Valid recoverable session: page refresh and opening a copied URL in a new tab work within supported session lifetime.
5. HTTP 401 token expiry: refresh only if supported and safe; otherwise login redirect.
6. HTTP 403 role denial: no privilege escalation, no infinite redirect or refresh loop.
7. Temporary Worker outage or timeout: bounded safe connection/retry state, no protected content, no infinite spinner, no incorrect forced logout.
8. Cross-origin iframe/direct asset embed: real parent and child origin/session handoff is tested, not assumed.
9. React StrictMode: no duplicate database writes, subscriptions, or authentication requests caused by effect replay.
10. Production dist/index.html + hashed assets: the pre-render gate still works after minification and obfuscation.
11. Session deadline: verify server-side 24-hour enforcement separately or explicitly mark NOT VERIFIED.
12. Do not mark any test PASS without actually executing it; use NOT VERIFIED when the deployed environment or credentials are unavailable.

