36.2 SESSION TIMESTAMP NORMALIZATION — NO FALSE LOGOUTS
- Read the approved session-start value from the actual storage keys used by the existing login system; inspect BOTH localStorage and sessionStorage only where the original architecture already uses them.
- Recognize, when present in the approved original system: integer millisecond Unix timestamps, integer second Unix timestamps, numeric strings, ISO-8601 strings, and documented legacy date formats.
- Normalize valid timestamps to epoch milliseconds before computing the session deadline. Reject impossible dates, NaN, nonfinite values, and future timestamps beyond reasonable clock skew.
- Use a single shared parser in the auth service; never scatter contradictory Date.parse(), Number(), or new Date() assumptions through components.
- If the approved system has an absolute 24-hour session, calculate expiresAt = originalAuthenticatedSessionStart + 24*60*60*1000. Page reloads, React remounts, token refreshes, or new tabs MUST NOT reset that start time.
- Do not use legacy loginDate !== today as a new logout requirement when the approved system uses a 24-hour deadline. Preserve loginDate as metadata if needed.
- If no trustworthy start time exists, do NOT create a fresh 24-hour window. Use only a verified backend session-restoration/validation flow, otherwise treat the session as unrecoverable and redirect to login.
- A timestamp stored in the browser is not an authoritative security guarantee; server-side absolute expiry must be independently verified. If unverified, report SERVER-SIDE 24-HOUR ENFORCEMENT NOT VERIFIED.
- A short-lived Firebase ID token expiry is different from the absolute Prudle session expiry. Refresh only through an existing, verified flow; never invent a route or persist a refresh token for convenience.
- On transient network failure, preserve valid credentials and show only a safe bounded connection/retry state. On confirmed invalid/revoked session, clear only app-owned credentials and redirect.

