Use a shared in-flight promise or equivalent for identical concurrent reads, with a key including the authorized user/scope, collection, query, sort, cursor, and relevant options. Avoid duplicate useEffect fetches, stale responses, and StrictMode-induced redundant network activity. Cancel obsolete reads where supported; never cancel or automatically replay an uncertain write.
41.5 / 23.5 — COMPLETE AND ACCURATE PAGINATION
Preserve the exact verified Worker pagination contract and cursor semantics. Allow access to all matching records, including over 5,000 or 10,000, without arbitrary caps or silent truncation. Prefer fetching only the visible page when supported, but obtain all necessary pages for exports, totals, and calculations requiring complete data. Never present partial totals as final; distinguish loading, incomplete, error, and empty states.
41.6 / 23.6 — CACHING WITHOUT STALE OR UNAUTHORIZED DATA
Use short-lived, appropriately scoped caching only where compatible with the original real-time requirements. Invalidate or update affected caches after confirmed writes. Clear private cached data on logout or account/role changes. Do not persist sensitive records or credentials in new browser storage without explicit approval.
41.7 / 23.7 — FAST AND SAFE POST-SAVE UPDATES
After server-confirmed create/update/delete, update affected React state and derived totals immediately, then perform a targeted authoritative refresh if needed. Preserve filters, sort, pagination, export correctness, and error rollback. Never reload the entire database unnecessarily or show success before confirmation.
41.8 / 23.8 — REACT AND BUNDLE PERFORMANCE
Keep initial JavaScript execution lightweight. Avoid expensive synchronous transforms during mount, unnecessary re-renders, duplicate subscriptions, and repeated full-array calculations. Lazy-load optional heavy features (for example PDF-generation libraries) only when doing so preserves the original behavior. Preserve original CSS, responsiveness, English-default/Arabic-switch requirements, and every existing function. Do not introduce extra production files beyond the approved build constraints without necessity and approval.
41.9 / 23.9 — FINITE LOADING AND FAILURE STATES
Do not leave a blank page or endless spinner. Use a lightweight loading state while the authorized application initializes, with bounded network timeouts, safe retry for reads, and actionable error messages. A temporary Worker/network failure must not be mistaken for an invalid session. When no valid recoverable session exists in a protected app, redirect directly to the approved login URL before showing protected UI.
41.10 / 23.10 — MEASUREMENT AND ACCEPTANCE
Before delivery, inspect Chrome DevTools Network and Performance (or equivalent) on cold load, warm load, filter selection, and repeat navigation. Report initial render time, total /db request count, duplicate requests, largest/slowest requests, and whether any sequential waterfall remains. Verify desktop/mobile, all records, totals, PDF, both languages, role permissions, and saved-data correctness. Do not promise a numeric speedup without measurements.
FINAL PERFORMANCE PRIORITY
Performance optimizations must never weaken authentication, change the original design, remove functionality, invent Worker endpoints, lose records, or silently alter data semantics. Fast initial UI and complete correct data are both mandatory. The current deployed Worker contract and supplied original source remain authoritative.
